  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how LCMV infection drives B cell loss through several converging immune pathways. At the top, infection of a myeloid cell leads to viral replication and type I interferon production, which then branches into multiple downstream effects.\n\nOne branch shows induction of CCL2, recruitment of inflammatory monocytes, and nitric oxide production; this pathway contributes to B cell apoptosis. A second branch highlights T cell activation: type I interferon promotes CD8+ T cell expansion and cytotoxic differentiation, and these T cells kill B cells through a perforin-mediated mechanism, again feeding into B cell apoptosis. A third branch involves dendritic cells and cytokines such as IL-10 and TNF, which push B cells to differentiate into short-lived plasma cells rather than persist in a normal B cell state. This altered differentiation also appears linked to the overall depletion of B cells.\n\nTaken together, the diagram presents B cell apoptosis as the central outcome of acute viral infection–induced immune activation. Rather than a single cause, the figure emphasizes coordinated action by monocytes, T cells, dendritic cells, inflammatory mediators, and interferon-driven signals that collectively reduce the B cell compartment and favor short-lived plasma cell responses.",
